#: processes/crdm-requirements-definition.bpmn:15
#. Diagram label
msgid "BA / Feature Requestor"
msgstr ""

#: processes/crdm-requirements-definition.bpmn:79
#. Diagram label
msgid "BA approves?"
msgstr ""

#: processes/crdm-requirements-definition.bpmn:36
#. Diagram element documentation
msgid "Business-process analysis here means reading the existing diagrams under processes/ and drawing the gap, so the skill that implements this step is the BPMN one."
msgstr ""

#: processes/crdm-requirements-definition.bpmn:10
#. Diagram label
msgid "CRDM Phases 2–4 — BPA and requirements"
msgstr ""

#: processes/crdm-requirements-definition.bpmn:13
#. Diagram element documentation
msgid "Map the current workflow, then define the requirements and the impact they carry, and loop until approved. Same shape as Phase 1 and for the same reason: an unapproved requirement is a proposal."
msgstr ""

#: processes/crdm-requirements-definition.bpmn:20
#. Diagram element documentation
msgid "Owns Phases 2 through 4a end to end, but the revise loop (GW_Reqs' 'revise' branch) re-enters at A_DefineReqs rather than back at A_MapWorkflow — so mapping the current workflow is this lane's one-time fact-finding, done once per phase run, while defining requirements and comparing options is what gets redone against BA feedback until Lane_Stakeholders approves."
msgstr ""

#: processes/crdm-requirements-definition.bpmn:34
#. Diagram label
msgid "Phase 2: Map current workflow (BPA)"
msgstr ""

#: processes/crdm-requirements-definition.bpmn:53
#. Diagram element documentation
msgid "Phase 4 often ends with a CHOICE rather than a plan, and this is the step that hands it over. Per option: what it does, its pro, its con, what it changes DOWNSTREAM, and how reversible it is — laid out where the rows can be read against each other, then one recommendation and a stated default. A step of its own rather than a line inside A_DefineReqs, because it has a different output and a different reader: the impact analysis is for the record, the comparison is the thing the BA answers from. Folding it in is how it gets skipped — the analysis feels finished, so the options go over as a list of names. Skipped legitimately when the analysis yields ONE viable approach. Do not manufacture alternatives to fill a table."
msgstr ""

#: processes/crdm-requirements-definition.bpmn:51
#. Diagram label
msgid "Phase 4a: Compare the viable options"
msgstr ""

#: processes/crdm-requirements-definition.bpmn:33
#. Diagram label
msgid "Phase begins"
msgstr ""

#: processes/crdm-requirements-definition.bpmn:80
#. Diagram label
msgid "Phase complete"
msgstr ""

#: processes/crdm-requirements-definition.bpmn:41
#. Diagram label
msgid "Phases 3–4: Define requirements + impact"
msgstr ""

#: processes/crdm-requirements-definition.bpmn:69
#. Diagram label
msgid "Review requirements and impact analysis"
msgstr ""

#: processes/crdm-requirements-definition.bpmn:87
#. Diagram label
msgid "revise"
msgstr ""

#: processes/crdm-requirements-definition.bpmn:28
#. Diagram label
msgid "Stakeholders"
msgstr ""

#: processes/crdm-requirements-definition.bpmn:77
#. Diagram element documentation
msgid "Stakeholders approve the requirements and impact analysis on the issue, or send them back for revision. Only their approval moves the work to sign-off; an agent never records an approval on a person's behalf."
msgstr ""

#: processes/crdm-requirements-definition.bpmn:71
#. Diagram element documentation
msgid "The BA reviews the formal requirements and the impact analysis the agent produced."
msgstr ""

#: processes/crdm-requirements-definition.bpmn:16
#. Diagram element documentation
msgid "The one checkpoint between the agent's comparison and the stakeholders' sign-off: BA_ReviewReqs is where someone who can read the impact analysis on its own technical terms vouches for it, because Lane_Stakeholders reviews only at defined checkpoints and needs the requirement already in a form they can approve without re-deriving it."
msgstr ""

#: processes/crdm-requirements-definition.bpmn:29
#. Diagram element documentation
msgid "The verdict GW_Reqs reads to decide between ending the phase and sending A_DefineReqs around again — a narrower approval than crdm-close.bpmn's final feature sign-off, since what is being approved here is the REQUIREMENT model itself, not that the delivered feature satisfies it."
msgstr ""

#: processes/crdm-requirements-definition.bpmn:43
#. Diagram element documentation
msgid "Two skills, because this step is two things: the requirements come from the CRDM process, the impact half is a dependency question the content graph answers."
msgstr ""

#: processes/crdm-requirements-definition.bpmn:88
#. Diagram label
msgid "yes"
msgstr ""
